  "e_honest_verdict": {
    "sub_goal_1_per_site_adaptive": "NOT a clean Pareto win over global-h. It helps only at the STRINGENT 3% operating point (validates +1 episode, BARC: 10/12 vs 9/12, at a LOWER held-out FPR 0.028 vs 0.030). At 5%/7% it TIES on episode count (10/12) at marginally lower FPR but with somewhat shorter median lead (34.8 vs 37.2 d @5%). Net: a modest, operating-point-dependent gain; its clearest value is tightening the FPR (matches at <= global FPR at every level, robust under the temporal single-run split).",
    "sub_goal_2_expanded_set": "Event set grown from 12->15 real NEON DO-hypoxia episodes (+BLWA, +SUGG, +PRLA) and 10->17 clean controls (+POSE,PRPO,REDB,TECR,TOMB,WALK,WLOU). CUSUM catches 11/15 at ~5% held-out FPR (lead 36.1 d) vs baseline 5/15 (25.5 d): the matched-FPR Pareto win HOLDS and STRENGTHENS at all swept FPR on BOTH splits. Of the 3 new episodes, BLWA is a genuine new validated detection (36.1 d lead, all precursor guardrails pass); SUGG and PRLA are UN-VALIDATABLE by construction (short records leave ZERO covered non-hypoxia-near pre-onset windows >=7 d before onset), so no detector can catch them and they penalize neither -- effectively 11/13 catchable.",
    "fpr_caveat": "MART absolute-FPR caveat FIXED via the temporal single-run split (each single-clean-run control calibrates & evaluates on disjoint halves of its OWN run -> control_sites_with_cal_hold_overlap==0, fully out-of-sample held-out FPR). Expanding controls 10->17 also dilutes any single-site window reuse. Pareto verdict identical.",
    "overall": "The central lead-time claim is REINFORCED on more real events: rolling-CUSUM remains a matched-FPR Pareto win (11/15 vs 5/15 @5%). Per-site adaptation is a real but modest refinement (helps at tight FPR, tightens the FPR caveat) rather than a decisive improvement."
  },
